{
 "fps": 30,
 "total": 21339,
 "xf": 18,
 "titleAfter": 5,
 "shots": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_01.jpg",
   "start": 0,
   "end": 333,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Peru, the coast, one of the driest deserts on Earth. And buried in the sand here is a ring of stone that may be five thousand five hundred years old.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s01.mp3",
     "dur": 10.032,
     "start": 10,
     "end": 311
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "T",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_02.jpg",
   "start": 315,
   "end": 653,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Older than Stonehenge. Older than the pyramids of Giza. Some websites say it was built by a lost civilization, or by visitors from the sky.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s02.mp3",
     "dur": 10.176,
     "start": 325,
     "end": 631
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_03.jpg",
   "start": 635,
   "end": 915,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "So tonight we keep a dig diary. Day by day, trench by trench, we ask what is really in the ground at Sechin Bajo.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s03.mp3",
     "dur": 8.256,
     "start": 645,
     "end": 893
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 4,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_04.jpg",
   "start": 897,
   "end": 1028,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Day one. Arrival. Let us begin with where we are.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s04.mp3",
     "dur": 3.288,
     "start": 907,
     "end": 1006
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 5,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_05.jpg",
   "start": 1010,
   "end": 1348,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "And what they found here in two thousand eight changed the timeline of the Americas. Or did it?",
     "file": "ep/audio/s05.mp3",
     "dur": 6.192,
     "start": 1020,
     "end": 1206
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 6,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": [
    "I \u00b7 ARRIVAL",
    "Day 1",
    "CASMA VALLEY \u00b7 ANCASH \u00b7 PERU"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_06.jpg",
   "start": 1330,
   "end": 1746,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Chapter one. Arrival. The Casma valley lies in the Ancash region of northern Peru, about three hundred kilometres north of Lima, and about twelve kilometres from the Pacific coast.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s06.mp3",
     "dur": 12.792,
     "start": 1340,
     "end": 1724
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 7,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_07.jpg",
   "start": 1728,
   "end": 2089,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Here it almost never rains. Less than ten millimetres a year. Life hugs the river, in narrow green oases that cut across the sand like green threads.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s07.mp3",
     "dur": 10.944,
     "start": 1738,
     "end": 2067
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 8,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_08.jpg",
   "start": 2071,
   "end": 2371,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Fifty-seven rivers cross the Peruvian coast. Each one carried water to the desert, and each valley became a place where people could settle.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s08.mp3",
     "dur": 8.904,
     "start": 2081,
     "end": 2349
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 9,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_09.jpg",
   "start": 2353,
   "end": 2750,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Today the valley holds a cluster of ancient sites. Cerro Sechin. Sechin Alto. Taukachi-Konkan. And the one we came for, Sechin Bajo, on the far bank of the river.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s09.mp3",
     "dur": 12.144,
     "start": 2363,
     "end": 2728
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 10,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_10.jpg",
   "start": 2732,
   "end": 3166,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The first big name here is the Peruvian archaeologist Julio Tello. On the first of July, nineteen thirty-seven, he and Toribio Mejia Xesspe found the carved stones of Cerro Sechin.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s10.mp3",
     "dur": 13.392,
     "start": 2742,
     "end": 3144
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 11,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_11.jpg",
   "start": 3148,
   "end": 3511,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Tello thought it was the capital of a whole culture. We now call it the Casma-Sechin culture. And for decades, the story stopped there, around sixteen hundred B C.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s11.mp3",
     "dur": 11.016,
     "start": 3158,
     "end": 3489
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 12,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_12.jpg",
   "start": 3493,
   "end": 3732,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Then, in the nineteen-nineties, a team started digging deeper at Sechin Bajo. Deeper than anyone had.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s12.mp3",
     "dur": 6.888,
     "start": 3503,
     "end": 3710
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 13,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": [
    "II \u00b7 TRENCH ONE",
    "Days 2 to 9",
    "LAYERS \u00b7 STONE \u00b7 ADOBE"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_13.jpg",
   "start": 3714,
   "end": 4134,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Chapter two. Trench one. The team was led by the German-Peruvian archaeologist Peter Fuchs. Their method was the oldest one there is: dig a square, record each layer, and go down.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s13.mp3",
     "dur": 12.912,
     "start": 3724,
     "end": 4112
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 14,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_14.jpg",
   "start": 4116,
   "end": 4444,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Each layer is a page in a book. Ash from a hearth. Sand blown in by wind. Clay, laid by hand. The deeper you go, the older it gets.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s14.mp3",
     "dur": 9.864,
     "start": 4126,
     "end": 4422
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 15,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_15.jpg",
   "start": 4426,
   "end": 4763,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Under the later buildings, they found a wall. Curved. Made of stone, and of rectangular adobe bricks. Older than the pyramid they were standing next to.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s15.mp3",
     "dur": 10.152,
     "start": 4436,
     "end": 4741
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 16,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_16.jpg",
   "start": 4745,
   "end": 5014,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "As they cleared the sand, the curve closed into a circle. A sunken circular plaza, ten to twelve metres across.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s16.mp3",
     "dur": 7.872,
     "start": 4755,
     "end": 4992
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 17,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_17.jpg",
   "start": 4996,
   "end": 5308,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Not a house. Not a grave. A public space. A place where people gathered, and where someone planned the shape before the first stone was laid.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s17.mp3",
     "dur": 9.312,
     "start": 5006,
     "end": 5286
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 18,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_18.jpg",
   "start": 5290,
   "end": 5597,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The stones are not giant. They are fitted by hand, bound with mud. This matters, because the internet loves to call it megalithic.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s18.mp3",
     "dur": 9.144,
     "start": 5300,
     "end": 5575
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 19,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_19.jpg",
   "start": 5579,
   "end": 5937,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Real building needs real people: someone to carry stone, someone to mix mud, someone to plan. That is already a society, even if we know nothing about its name.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s19.mp3",
     "dur": 10.848,
     "start": 5589,
     "end": 5915
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 20,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": [
    "III \u00b7 DATING",
    "Days 10 to 14",
    "CARBON \u00b7 CHARCOAL \u00b7 3500 BCE"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_20.jpg",
   "start": 5919,
   "end": 6097,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Chapter three. Dating. How do we know how old a wall is? We ask the charcoal.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s20.mp3",
     "dur": 4.848,
     "start": 5929,
     "end": 6075
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 21,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_21.jpg",
   "start": 6079,
   "end": 6372,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Every living thing takes in carbon. When it dies, a tiny part of it decays at a known rate. A lab can measure what is left.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s21.mp3",
     "dur": 8.688,
     "start": 6089,
     "end": 6350
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 22,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_22.jpg",
   "start": 6354,
   "end": 6696,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "At Sechin Bajo, the samples came from burned plant material in the layers of the plaza. Radiocarbon put them around thirty-five hundred B C.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s22.mp3",
     "dur": 10.32,
     "start": 6364,
     "end": 6674
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 23,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_23.jpg",
   "start": 6678,
   "end": 7016,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "That is about fifty-five hundred years ago. Five hundred years older than Caral, the famous city of Peru, built around twenty-six hundred B C.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s23.mp3",
     "dur": 10.2,
     "start": 6688,
     "end": 6994
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 24,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_24.jpg",
   "start": 6998,
   "end": 7246,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Older than the Great Pyramid of Giza by roughly nine hundred years. Older than Stonehenge's first ditch.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s24.mp3",
     "dur": 7.2,
     "start": 7008,
     "end": 7224
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 25,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_25.jpg",
   "start": 7228,
   "end": 7622,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "But careful. Radiocarbon gives a range, not one date. And the team itself announced these results in two thousand nine, in a journal. Not everyone has seen the full data yet.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s25.mp3",
     "dur": 12.048,
     "start": 7238,
     "end": 7600
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 26,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_26.jpg",
   "start": 7604,
   "end": 7950,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The report: Fuchs and colleagues, Boletin de Arqueologia, Catholic University of Peru, two thousand nine. It is a real paper, and a careful one.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s26.mp3",
     "dur": 10.464,
     "start": 7614,
     "end": 7928
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 27,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_27.jpg",
   "start": 7932,
   "end": 8250,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Some Andean specialists say: promising, but we need more dates from more layers. That is fair. One strong claim needs more than one trench.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s27.mp3",
     "dur": 9.528,
     "start": 7942,
     "end": 8228
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 28,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": [
    "IV \u00b7 THE FRIEZE",
    "Days 15 to 20",
    "RELIEF \u00b7 ART \u00b7 3600 BCE"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_28.jpg",
   "start": 8232,
   "end": 8553,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Chapter four. The frieze. Nearby, the team also reported a stone frieze, two metres tall, dated around thirty-six hundred B C.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s28.mp3",
     "dur": 9.624,
     "start": 8242,
     "end": 8531
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 29,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_29.jpg",
   "start": 8535,
   "end": 8812,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "If that date holds, it would be some of the earliest monumental art in the Americas. Before any pottery in this region.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s29.mp3",
     "dur": 8.136,
     "start": 8545,
     "end": 8790
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 30,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_30.jpg",
   "start": 8794,
   "end": 9120,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "That is the odd thing. At this time, people here did not make pots. Archaeologists call it the pre-ceramic period. Yet they built plazas.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s30.mp3",
     "dur": 9.768,
     "start": 8804,
     "end": 9098
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 31,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_31.jpg",
   "start": 9102,
   "end": 9388,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "They had baskets. They had cotton, and fishing nets. They fished on the coast and farmed in the valley. No pots, but great buildings.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s31.mp3",
     "dur": 8.448,
     "start": 9112,
     "end": 9366
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 32,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_32.jpg",
   "start": 9370,
   "end": 9761,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Now compare Cerro Sechin, just across the river, around sixteen hundred B C. A wall of upright granite slabs, carved with warriors, and the bodies of their victims.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s32.mp3",
     "dur": 11.952,
     "start": 9380,
     "end": 9739
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 33,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_33.jpg",
   "start": 9743,
   "end": 10102,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Dramatic art, almost two thousand years after Sechin Bajo. These are the carvings that appear in documentaries. They are not the oldest part of the story.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s33.mp3",
     "dur": 10.872,
     "start": 9753,
     "end": 10080
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 34,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_34.jpg",
   "start": 10084,
   "end": 10356,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Which is a trap for the casual viewer. Cerro Sechin is famous. Sechin Bajo is older and almost unknown.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s34.mp3",
     "dur": 7.968,
     "start": 10094,
     "end": 10334
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 35,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": [
    "V \u00b7 THE NEIGHBOURS",
    "Days 21 to 25",
    "SECHIN ALTO \u00b7 CARAL \u00b7 SCALE"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_35.jpg",
   "start": 10338,
   "end": 10699,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Chapter five. The neighbours. Look at Sechin Alto, right beside it. A temple platform three hundred and fifty metres by three hundred, thirty-five metres high.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s35.mp3",
     "dur": 10.944,
     "start": 10348,
     "end": 10677
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 36,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_36.jpg",
   "start": 10681,
   "end": 10977,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Five plazas stretch two kilometres. The whole complex covers three to four hundred hectares: five times the size of Caral.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s36.mp3",
     "dur": 8.784,
     "start": 10691,
     "end": 10955
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 37,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_37.jpg",
   "start": 10959,
   "end": 11243,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Inside, large stones alternate with small ones. Conical adobe bricks fill the core. Careful, ordered, planned work.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s37.mp3",
     "dur": 8.376,
     "start": 10969,
     "end": 11221
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 38,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_38.jpg",
   "start": 11225,
   "end": 11525,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Tello called it the largest building of the Americas. So when someone says: the people here were primitive, ask them to stand at its foot.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s38.mp3",
     "dur": 8.904,
     "start": 11235,
     "end": 11503
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 39,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_39.jpg",
   "start": 11507,
   "end": 11827,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The region also links to Caral, Norte Chico, a chain of early cities that grew along the Peruvian coast, with no writing and no wheels.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s39.mp3",
     "dur": 9.576,
     "start": 11517,
     "end": 11805
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 40,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_40.jpg",
   "start": 11809,
   "end": 12121,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "So Sechin Bajo is not alone. It is the oldest piece of a big landscape, one that grew in complexity over two thousand years.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s40.mp3",
     "dur": 9.312,
     "start": 11819,
     "end": 12099
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 41,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_41.jpg",
   "start": 12103,
   "end": 12345,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "And much of it is still under farmland and sand. Whole zones around Sechin Alto have never been dug.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s41.mp3",
     "dur": 6.984,
     "start": 12113,
     "end": 12323
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 42,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "T",
   "ch": [
    "VI \u00b7 THE CLAIM",
    "Days 26 to 29",
    "LOST CIVILIZATION \u00b7 ASTRONAUTS"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_42.jpg",
   "start": 12327,
   "end": 12677,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Chapter six. The claim. Search online, and you will find posts saying Sechin Bajo is proof of a lost civilization, or of ancient astronauts.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s42.mp3",
     "dur": 10.584,
     "start": 12337,
     "end": 12655
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 43,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "T",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_43.jpg",
   "start": 12659,
   "end": 12892,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The argument is always the same. Too old. Too big. Too perfect. So someone else must have built it.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s43.mp3",
     "dur": 6.672,
     "start": 12669,
     "end": 12870
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 44,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_44.jpg",
   "start": 12874,
   "end": 13153,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Take them one by one. Too old? The dates are real, from charcoal. Hammer stones, baskets, and mud were all this took.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s44.mp3",
     "dur": 8.232,
     "start": 12884,
     "end": 13131
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 45,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_45.jpg",
   "start": 13135,
   "end": 13422,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Too perfect? A circle is easy. A cord, a stake, and a person walking around it. Archaeologists have found cords like this.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s45.mp3",
     "dur": 8.496,
     "start": 13145,
     "end": 13400
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 46,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "D",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_46.jpg",
   "start": 13404,
   "end": 13749,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Anything alien? No object. No metal. No machine. No unexplained tool. Not one thing in the trench that people of that time could not have made.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s46.mp3",
     "dur": 10.416,
     "start": 13414,
     "end": 13727
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 47,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "D",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_47.jpg",
   "start": 13731,
   "end": 13977,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "What remains is a feeling, that ancient people could not have done it. That feeling says more about us than about them.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s47.mp3",
     "dur": 7.104,
     "start": 13741,
     "end": 13955
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 48,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_48.jpg",
   "start": 13959,
   "end": 14275,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "And it is also quietly unfair to the real builders, the people of the Peruvian coast, who invented monumental architecture on their own.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s48.mp3",
     "dur": 9.456,
     "start": 13969,
     "end": 14253
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 49,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": [
    "VII \u00b7 WHO WERE THEY?",
    "Days 30 to 33",
    "FISH \u00b7 COTTON \u00b7 CEREMONY"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_49.jpg",
   "start": 14257,
   "end": 14526,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Chapter seven. Who were they? Honestly, we do not know. We have no name, no language, no writing. Only what they built.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s49.mp3",
     "dur": 7.896,
     "start": 14267,
     "end": 14504
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 50,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_50.jpg",
   "start": 14508,
   "end": 14808,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Their food was anchovies from the sea, and beans and squash from the valley. Their trade was cotton, which they spun into nets and cloth.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s50.mp3",
     "dur": 8.904,
     "start": 14518,
     "end": 14786
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 51,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_51.jpg",
   "start": 14790,
   "end": 15077,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The circular plaza suggests rituals. A shared fire. A shared story. Maybe a ruler, maybe a council, maybe both.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s51.mp3",
     "dur": 8.496,
     "start": 14800,
     "end": 15055
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 52,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "T",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_52.jpg",
   "start": 15059,
   "end": 15313,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Some scholars think early temples here tracked the sun and the seasons. That is a theory, with hints, not proof.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s52.mp3",
     "dur": 7.368,
     "start": 15069,
     "end": 15291
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 53,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_53.jpg",
   "start": 15295,
   "end": 15576,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "We only know about one hundredth of the site. A future trench could change the story again. That is the thrill of this season.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s53.mp3",
     "dur": 8.28,
     "start": 15305,
     "end": 15554
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 54,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_54.jpg",
   "start": 15558,
   "end": 15927,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Back in the trench, nothing is thrown away. Every bucket of sand goes through a sieve, because the smallest thing, a bead, a seed, a fish bone, can tell us how people lived.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s54.mp3",
     "dur": 11.208,
     "start": 15568,
     "end": 15905
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 55,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_55.jpg",
   "start": 15909,
   "end": 16215,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "From those scraps, the team can count what people ate. Anchovies. Sea snails. Squash. A diet of the coast, not of the stars.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s55.mp3",
     "dur": 9.12,
     "start": 15919,
     "end": 16193
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 56,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_56.jpg",
   "start": 16197,
   "end": 16530,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "And every layer is drawn by hand, to scale, before it is removed. Once a layer is dug, it is gone forever. The drawing is all that is left.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s56.mp3",
     "dur": 10.032,
     "start": 16207,
     "end": 16508
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 57,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_57.jpg",
   "start": 16512,
   "end": 16839,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Evening in the camp. Wind. Dust in the lamp. This is the part of archaeology that documentaries skip: the slow, careful, quiet work.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s57.mp3",
     "dur": 9.816,
     "start": 16522,
     "end": 16817
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 58,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_58.jpg",
   "start": 16821,
   "end": 17152,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "One of the quiet treasures is cloth. Woven cotton, preserved by the dry desert for thousands of years. Ordinary people, with ordinary skill.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s58.mp3",
     "dur": 9.936,
     "start": 16831,
     "end": 17130
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 59,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_59.jpg",
   "start": 17134,
   "end": 17468,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The valley is still farmed today. The same river, the same sand, the same sun. That continuity is why the ground here keeps its secrets so well.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s59.mp3",
     "dur": 10.056,
     "start": 17144,
     "end": 17446
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 60,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "T",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_60.jpg",
   "start": 17450,
   "end": 17769,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Some videos claim the site is far older, tens of thousands of years. There is no radiocarbon date, no layer, and no paper that says so.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s60.mp3",
     "dur": 9.552,
     "start": 17460,
     "end": 17747
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 61,
   "fx": "sand",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_61.jpg",
   "start": 17751,
   "end": 18061,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "A good rule from the field: the further a claim goes beyond the data, the more careful you must be. Charcoal can be dated. A feeling cannot.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s61.mp3",
     "dur": 9.264,
     "start": 17761,
     "end": 18039
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 62,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_62.jpg",
   "start": 18043,
   "end": 18382,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "So here is the surprise. The real story is stranger than the myth. People with no pottery and no writing built public circles of stone, and it spread.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s62.mp3",
     "dur": 10.224,
     "start": 18053,
     "end": 18360
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 63,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_63.jpg",
   "start": 18364,
   "end": 18730,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "And the same idea, the sunken circular plaza, appears again and again along the coast, in Caral and beyond. A tradition, passed on for a thousand years.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s63.mp3",
     "dur": 11.112,
     "start": 18374,
     "end": 18708
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 64,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "E",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_64.jpg",
   "start": 18712,
   "end": 19014,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Today drones, ground radar and new dates are mapping the buried parts. Every year, the picture gets a little sharper, and a little older.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s64.mp3",
     "dur": 9.0,
     "start": 18722,
     "end": 18992
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 65,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "P",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_65.jpg",
   "start": 18996,
   "end": 19322,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "And that is what a diary like this is for. Not to find a shortcut to an answer, but to watch slowly, honestly, as people stand up out of the sand.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s65.mp3",
     "dur": 9.792,
     "start": 19006,
     "end": 19300
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 66,
   "fx": "score",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "V",
   "ch": [
    "VERDICT",
    "The Scorecard",
    "EVIDENCE VS THEORY"
   ],
   "img": "ep/img/shot_66.jpg",
   "start": 19304,
   "end": 19392,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Time to score Sechin Bajo.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s66.mp3",
     "dur": 1.848,
     "start": 19314,
     "end": 19370
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 67,
   "fx": "score",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "V",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_67.jpg",
   "start": 19374,
   "end": 19826,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "A circular plaza of stone and mud: evidence. Radiocarbon near thirty-five hundred B C: evidence. Oldest monumental building in the Americas: partial, since it needs more dates.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s67.mp3",
     "dur": 13.992,
     "start": 19384,
     "end": 19804
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 68,
   "fx": "score",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "V",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_68.jpg",
   "start": 19808,
   "end": 20118,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "A lost civilization or alien builders: no evidence. Impossible for ancient people: debunked. Who they were: still unknown.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s68.mp3",
     "dur": 9.24,
     "start": 19818,
     "end": 20096
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 69,
   "fx": "glow",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "V",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_69.jpg",
   "start": 20100,
   "end": 20515,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "The verdict. Real, and remarkable. Sechin Bajo is one of the oldest monuments in the Americas. And no aliens were needed. Just people, mud, stone, and a very good idea.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s69.mp3",
     "dur": 12.744,
     "start": 20110,
     "end": 20493
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 70,
   "fx": "dust",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_70.jpg",
   "start": 20497,
   "end": 20780,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Five and a half thousand years ago, someone pressed a stone into wet clay and began a circle. We are still reading what they started.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s70.mp3",
     "dur": 8.352,
     "start": 20507,
     "end": 20758
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 71,
   "fx": "stars",
   "motion": "in",
   "tag": "",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_71.jpg",
   "start": 20762,
   "end": 21049,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Next case: a stone city in Africa, a name that means houses of stone, and a question that colonial writers tried to bury.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s71.mp3",
     "dur": 8.496,
     "start": 20772,
     "end": 21027
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": 72,
   "fx": "none",
   "motion": "out",
   "tag": "",
   "ch": null,
   "img": "ep/img/shot_72.jpg",
   "start": 21031,
   "end": 21264,
   "lines": [
    {
     "who": "narrator",
     "text": "Would you have kept digging? Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.",
     "file": "ep/audio/s72.mp3",
     "dur": 6.672,
     "start": 21041,
     "end": 21242
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}